    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thareed is a dish of contrasts: "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "slow-braising"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "slow-braised"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " lamb that falls apart in a deeply spiced broth, ladled over torn flatbread using the "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "bread-soaking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "bread-soaking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " technique so that the bread becomes part of the dish rather than a vehicle for it. The oldest layer on the plate should be the most flavoured."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tip: use a day-old flatbread if possible. Stale bread absorbs broth better than fresh without going to mush."
          }
        ]
      },
